{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And you shall not lie with your neighbor's wife, to cast your seed [for sowing]</b>. Rabbi Abraham [Ibn Ezra] (on Leviticus 18:20) said that the act of intercourse is divided into three parts: one for procreation and propagation; the second to relieve the body's heat; and the third for desire, which resembles the desire of beasts. And Scripture says 'לְזָרַע' ('for seed'), and his intent is: even for seed, and behold, she is entirely forbidden. And it is possible that it says 'לְזָרַע' to mention the reason for the prohibition, for the seed would not be known to whom it belongs, and from this great abominations and evils would come to both of them. And it did not mention this in the punishment (Leviticus 20:18), for even if he had intercourse with her without emitting seed he is liable. Therefore concerning the sotah it says (Numbers 5:13) 'and a man lies with her with an emission of seed,' for on account of the seed his jealousy will be aroused. And likewise in the case of a betrothed bondmaid (Leviticus 19:20) it mentions an emission of seed, for the prohibition is on account of his begetting seed from the bondmaid. But the correct interpretation in my eyes is that since his neighbor's wife is entirely forbidden to him, he who touches her shall not go unpunished (Proverbs 10:29) — it was necessary to say 'לְזָרַע,' for if it had said only 'לֹא תִתֵּן שְׁכָבְתְּךָ' ('you shall not cast your lying'), it would appear that it warns even against lying with her to embrace and kiss, whereas here, in matters subject to excision, it warns; therefore it was necessary to mention lying for seed, to specify that the warning concerns the act of intercourse. And this is the reason in the case of a betrothed bondmaid, who is like a married woman. And likewise it says concerning a married woman (Leviticus 20:10), 'who commits adultery with his neighbor's wife,' and does not say 'lies,' for the warning is not against mere lying. And here it does not say 'to uncover her nakedness,' for it mentions that expression only in the case of flesh-relatives and in the case of a menstruant, where the prohibition is on account of the uncovering, as it says (Leviticus 20:19), 'for he has uncovered his flesh-relative.' And our Sages derived in the case of a married woman [from the word לְזָרַע] to exclude one who engages in intercourse with a dead organ, and they said concerning a betrothed bondmaid that he is not liable unless he completes the act of penetration, as is explained in Yevamot (55a)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:55:37.471967134Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}